  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 AM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy fog possible this morning. A gradual \nwarming trend is expected for the remainder of the week with dry\nconditions continuing.\n\nSurface high pressure remains dominant over the local area with an \nupper ridging building over eastern US and a closed low forms near \nthe eastern Gulf coast today. Patchy fog is possible this\nmorning and will dissipate by mid morning. The building ridge \nis allowing a gradual warming trend to continue through \nSaturday. Temperatures today will reach the lower 60 after a \nchilly below freezing start for most. Near to below freezing \ntemperatures are forecast again tonight for most. High \ntemperatures Thursday will be a few degrees warmer in the mid \n60s and Friday warmer so in the upper 60s to near 70F. This \nweekend, temperatures level out some with highs in the upper 60s\nto near 70F. Thursday night through the weekend lows will be \nabove freezing. Winds remain light this week with the high in \ncontrol.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above average temperatures persist this weekend with \ndry conditions continuing on Saturday. There is a chance for rain on \nSunday, though confidence in rainfall is low (especially inland).\n\nThe upper ridge gradually breaks down by the end of the week, while \nthe Gulf Coast low slowly tracks NE over the weekend. The models \ncontinue to show a great deal of  uncertainty with the track of the \nlow, primarily showing two tracks: the GFS brings the low further \ninland and over the local area and the ECMWF/CMC keeps the low \noffshore following up the Carolina coasts. The difference in the \ntrack will determine how much (if any) rainfall is received locally. \nThe model ensembles are favoring an offshore track, but the spread \nin rainfall totals remains high. The GEFS/EPS ensemble member total \nQPF shows little to no rain west of I-95 and 0.50-1.00\" east of I-\n95. The 75th percentile shows 0.50-1.25\" across most of the area. \nThe highest amounts will likely occur along the coast with the \nlowest amounts in the piedmont. Overall, the trend in rainfall has \nincreased some, but confidence remains low. Unfortunately, \nconfidence in an appreciable wetting rain remains low west of I-95 \nat this time. Highs remain in the 60s with lows in the 40s.",
